   \code{"RIL"} \tab Two founders -> F1 -> single-seed descent to F\emph{t} \tab \code{lines}, \code{generation} \cr
   \code{"BCsFt"} \tab Two founders -> F1 -> \emph{s} backcrosses to founder 1 -> self to BC\emph{s}F\emph{t} \tab \code{lines}, \code{backcrosses}, \code{generation} \cr
   \code{"NAM"} \tab One common founder x \code{families} donors -> RILs in each family \tab \code{lines}, \code{families}, \code{generation} \cr
   \code{"AB-NAM"} \tab As NAM, with \emph{s} backcrosses to the common parent in each family \tab \code{lines}, \code{families}, \code{backcrosses}, \code{generation} \cr
   \code{"MAGIC"} \tab 4 or 8 founders intermated in a funnel -> self to F\emph{t} \tab \code{lines}, \code{founders}, \code{generation} \cr
}


\code{lines} is always the total number of lines, so designs with the same
\code{lines}, \code{locations} and \code{reps} use the same field plots and compare fairly.
F1 is generation 1: F2 is \code{generation = 2}, an F6 RIL is \code{generation = 6},
and BC1F3 is \verb{backcrosses = 1, generation = 3} (one backcross, then two selfings),
matching the convention of breedsim's \code{mapping-program}.
