\section{QTL effect and genetic model}{

Traits are additive. \code{qtl_pve} is the share of single-plot phenotypic
variance the QTL would explain in a biparental RIL in which it segregates,
so its allele effect is the same in every design and realized variance
explained differs by design (it is reported as \code{realized_pve}). In NAM and
AB-NAM, donors carry the QTL allele with probability \code{qtl_donor_freq}; in
MAGIC every founder does. A polygenic background of \code{n_background} loci
gives the plot-basis heritability \code{plot_h2}, and genotype-by-location
variance is \code{gxe_ratio} times the genetic variance, so more locations and
reps sharpen line means.
}

\section{Detection}{

A replicate counts as a detection when the LOD within \code{window} cM of the
QTL exceeds the genome-wide threshold (permutations, or \code{threshold}).
\code{other_peaks} records whether any other chromosome also exceeds it. With a
polygenic background these are mostly real background loci (linked small
effects add up in segments that recombination has not broken), so
\code{other_peaks} rises with population size; with \code{n_background = 0} it is a
genome-wide false-positive rate, which the permutation threshold holds
near \code{alpha}. See
\code{\link[=qtl_scan]{qtl_scan()}} for the models.
}

\section{Costs and time}{

\code{cost} adds crosses, single-seed-descent plant generations, genotyping and
field plots at the unit costs given (placeholders: replace them with your
own). \code{years} is generations divided by \code{generations_per_year}, rounded up,
plus one year for the field trial. \code{max_plots}, \code{max_cost} and \code{max_years}
make designs over budget infeasible, so tuners skip them.
}

